# Security rules

- Treat all user, network, file, database, webhook, and third-party data as untrusted.
- Enforce authorization server-side on every protected resource and action.
- Use safe framework APIs for HTML, SQL, shell, paths, URLs, redirects, and templates; never concatenate untrusted input into executable contexts.
- Protect state-changing browser requests against CSRF where relevant.
- Use secure cookie attributes and short-lived, scoped credentials.
- Validate file type, size, name, storage path, and access controls for uploads.
- Do not log secrets, tokens, passwords, session identifiers, payment data, or unnecessary personal data.
- Apply rate limits and abuse controls to authentication, password reset, invitations, search, exports, and expensive endpoints.
- Keep dependencies patched and avoid adding abandoned packages for security-sensitive tasks.
- For security-sensitive changes, invoke the security-auditor subagent and resolve critical/high findings before completion.
